You must produce one entry for each field below. The fields are INDEPENDENT:
decide each one strictly on its own definition, even if it seems unrelated to
this page. Each entry has three parts, `applicable`, `value`, `quote`, decided
in this order, field by field:

1. `applicable`: does THIS page bear on the field at all? A document spans many
   pages; a field about grading weights, a disability policy, or rubric levels
   may simply not appear on this page. If the page says nothing about what the
   field asks for, set `applicable=false`, leave `value` at its empty default
   (false, 0, "", []), and `quote=""`. Only when the page DOES speak to the field
   do you set `applicable=true` and judge the value. (This matters: a feature
   stated on one page must not be marked missing just because other pages are
   silent, those pages are simply not applicable.)
2. `value` (only meaningful when applicable): decide it from the page's evidence
   alone, never from the field name, the document title, the language of the
   page, or what you expect the document to be about. Some fields ask whether a
   GOOD feature is present (an explicit weight, a stated policy, a defined
   level): if the page shows it, the value is TRUE, do not default to false out
   of caution. Others ask whether a PROBLEM is present (a spelling slip, an
   off-topic page): the value is true only when you can point to the concrete
   problem. Read closely rather than assuming, a tidy page is not evidence a
   required feature is absent, and a clean first impression is not evidence of
   correctness. A proper noun, an institution name, a formula, or a correctly
   spelled foreign-language term is NOT an error. When you genuinely cannot tell
   whether a feature reaches the bar the field asks for, it is more useful to
   record it as NOT met (false / the empty default) and let the rule engine
   surface it than to give it the benefit of the doubt, these facts exist to
   find problems, not to excuse them.
3. `quote`: the quote is the verbatim proof that backs your value, at most 12
   words copied word-for-word from this page, never paraphrased. The test for a
   good quote: a reader who saw ONLY the quote should be able to see why the value
   is what it is. If the span does not, on its own, demonstrate the value, leave
   the quote empty rather than approximating. How you fill it depends on what you
   are attesting:
   - You found a concrete thing ON the page (a feature that IS present, or a
     problem that IS present such as a misspelled word): `applicable=true` and
     `quote` is the exact span that shows it, the sentence that states the policy,
     poses the question, defines the level, or contains the misspelling. A
     non-empty quote is REQUIRED here.
   - The page speaks to the field but the thing the field looks for is ABSENT
     (e.g. the field asks for an explicit weight, a stated policy, a grading
     criterion, or a defined level, and this page is squarely where it would
     appear yet does not state it): set `applicable=true`, `value` to the empty
     default (false / 0 / "" / []), and `quote=""`. An absence has NO verbatim
     span. Do NOT fill the quote with a nearby title, a section heading, a task
     name, or the topic, those name what the page is about, they do not show the
     missing thing. A heading like "HOMEWORK: PRODUCT LIFE CYCLE OBSERVATION" is
     never proof that a grading criterion or an AI policy is missing; for such an
     absence the only honest quote is the empty string.
   - The page simply has no bearing on the field at all: `applicable=false`,
     empty default value, `quote=""` (see step 1).

