Rules:
- Judge only THIS page; do not assume content from other pages or the title.
- Do not invent content that is not on the page.
- A non-empty quote must be verbatim and must, on its own, demonstrate the value.
  Never paste a sentence that is merely on the same topic, and never quote a title,
  a heading, or a task/section name as proof, those say what the page is about,
  not whether the rule is met. A feature that IS present, or a concrete problem
  that IS present, requires such a self-evident quote; an ABSENCE is proved by an
  empty quote, not by a topical span or a heading (see step 3).
- Spelling and grammar problems are often subtle: a compound word wrongly split
  into two (or two words wrongly joined), a single missing or swapped letter
  inside otherwise-correct text, a wrong inflection. Check each word on its own
  merits rather than trusting the page's overall tidy impression.
- NEVER treat a proper name as a spelling or grammar error. Names of people,
  universities, departments, institutions, companies, brands, products, projects,
  cities, countries, or other places are spelled however their owner spells them, they are not dictionary words and an unusual spelling or capitalisation is NOT a
  mistake. Examples of what to IGNORE for the spelling check: "UNIVERSITY OF
  LIPER", "Rede Luss", a surname, a course code, an acronym, a logo caption. The
  same applies to formulas, variable names, units, and correctly spelled
  foreign-language terms. Only flag a misspelling in ordinary prose words.
- Language checks judge the AUTHOR'S OWN prose only. Do not flag: source code,
  identifiers, or URLs; text visible inside an embedded screenshot, news
  clipping, or other reproduced third-party material (its wording belongs to the
  third party, not the author); or the institutional logo/header repeated on
  every page. For that logo especially: the transcription often garbles it, read it from the image, and if the image shows it spelled normally, it is NOT
  an error. Never quote a correctly spelled span as proof of a language error:
  if the exact wrong characters are not in your quote, do not report it.

